1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,055
On May twentieth, nineteen sixty-four,
according to the Atomic Energy

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,295
Commission's official annual account,
engineers in the Nevada desert reached

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:13,055
a remarkable milestone. They brought
a nuclear-powered ramjet called Tory

4
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:17,375
Two-C to full power. Air was driven
through its reactor under conditions

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:21,455
intended to represent a missile
traveling at Mach two point eight, near

6
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:25,335
sea level. And the official government
account of the test did not call it a

7
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:28,540
disappointment. It called
the May trials successful.

8
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:33,155
Now jump forward just forty-two days.
On July first, the Defense Department

9
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:37,215
informed the Atomic Energy Commission
that it would not pursue a flight-test

10
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:41,780
objective for Project Pluto. Further
planned reactor tests were canceled.

11
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:44,455
The desert facility
would be mothballed.

12
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,860
Yet the missile that was
supposed to carry it never flew.

13
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:53,015
How can one of the most ambitious
propulsion projects of the Cold War pass

14
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,520
its decisive reactor test
and still be abandoned?

15
00:00:56,980 --> 00:01:00,755
The answer begins with a distinction
that is easy to miss in surviving

16
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:03,880
photographs. There was
a tested reactor engine.

17
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:07,840
And there was an imagined weapon.
Only one of them became real.

18
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:12,615
To understand why, we have to enter a
period when the American military was

19
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:16,455
willing to consider an idea almost
impossible to separate from the fears of

20
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,575
its time: a missile that did not need
to carry conventional fuel for its

21
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,580
long cruise. That idea
was Project Pluto.

22
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,755
And its most important achievement
would also expose the distance between

23
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:31,540
proving a machine could function and
proving that the world needed it.

24
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:34,930
Pluto proposed a radically
different source of heat.

25
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:39,910
Inside its engine, a nuclear reactor
would transfer energy to incoming air.

26
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:44,005
That heated air would expand and rush
rearward, creating thrust without the

27
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:47,270
same onboard fuel supply a
chemical-burning jet required.

28
00:01:47,830 --> 00:01:51,885
It was a nuclear ramjet. And a ramjet
has another important characteristic:

29
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:56,730
it works by using the speed of the
vehicle itself to compress incoming air.

30
00:01:57,070 --> 00:02:00,685
It is not an ordinary engine that can
simply take off from a runway under

31
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:03,590
its own power. So imagine
two separate machines.

32
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:08,225
One is the reactor and air passage:
the power-producing heart of the

33
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:12,685
proposed engine. This is what Livermore
eventually built and operated during

34
00:02:12,710 --> 00:02:17,565
ground tests. The other is SLAM,
the proposed Supersonic Low-Altitude

35
00:02:17,590 --> 00:02:21,690
Missile. The reactor did not solve
all of those requirements by itself.

36
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:26,885
The attraction was endurance. The Pluto
concept moved the energy source into

37
00:02:26,910 --> 00:02:30,925
a nuclear reactor. In principle,
it offered an entirely different balance

38
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:33,210
between fuel carried and time aloft.

39
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:36,550
But theoretical endurance
was not unlimited range.

40
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:40,190
A complete flying vehicle would
still have faced engineering limits,

41
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:43,250
guidance requirements,
and an operational mission.

42
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:46,965
You can see machines, test rigs,
technicians, and equipment developed

43
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:50,990
around the proposed SLAM system.
They are genuine period research,

44
00:02:51,070 --> 00:02:53,730
but they are not footage of
a completed missile flying.

45
00:02:54,310 --> 00:02:58,205
That distinction matters. When a
model survives a wind-tunnel test,

46
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:00,970
it tells engineers
something about airflow.

47
00:03:01,230 --> 00:03:05,225
It says nothing by itself about whether
a nuclear reactor can survive the

48
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:09,045
same mission. When a reactor
produces heat under simulated flight

49
00:03:09,070 --> 00:03:11,450
conditions, it answers
a different question.

50
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:15,310
It cannot prove that an entire
autonomous weapon has been developed.

51
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:18,310
Project Pluto needed
both sets of answers.

52
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:20,885
The engine would go much
further than the missile.

53
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:25,425
And the reason engineers pursued such
a difficult design had less to do with

54
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:29,005
a love of strange machinery than with
the strategic problem Washington was

55
00:03:29,030 --> 00:03:30,490
trying to solve.

56
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:32,970
Go back to the late nineteen-fifties.

57
00:03:33,330 --> 00:03:37,250
The Cold War had turned military
technology into a contest of distance,

58
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:41,910
speed, warning time, and the ability
to survive an opponent's defenses.

59
00:03:42,590 --> 00:03:46,270
Long-range bombers remained central
to American nuclear strategy.

60
00:03:47,010 --> 00:03:50,725
Ballistic missiles were developing
rapidly, but the shape of the future

61
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:55,345
arsenal had not yet been settled. This
was the world in which Project Pluto

62
00:03:55,370 --> 00:03:59,585
began at Lawrence Radiation Laboratory
in nineteen fifty-seven, working for

63
00:03:59,610 --> 00:04:02,930
the Atomic Energy Commission
and the United States Air Force.

64
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:07,305
Planners were exploring a machine that
might cruise at supersonic speed and

65
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:11,725
low altitude, presenting an unusual
challenge to defenses designed around

66
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:14,890
bombers and missiles
following more familiar paths.

67
00:04:15,010 --> 00:04:19,205
Low altitude was an ambition, not
proof that such a weapon could travel

68
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:24,210
anywhere unseen. Terrain,
navigation, structural loads,

69
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:26,265
and the realities of
detection still mattered.

70
00:04:26,290 --> 00:04:30,325
The potential attraction was that a
nuclear heat source would not consume

71
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:33,170
conventional jet fuel in
the way a bomber engine did.

72
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:36,770
But potential range was only
one part of a weapons system.

73
00:04:37,430 --> 00:04:41,410
The Air Force already had air-launched
missiles such as the Hound Dog.

74
00:04:41,870 --> 00:04:46,185
A B-fifty-two carrying one of those
weapons represented an actual operating

75
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:50,645
approach to long-range attack. At the
same time, programs such as Atlas and

76
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:54,625
Minuteman were demonstrating a
different idea: sending a payload on a

77
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:58,630
ballistic trajectory instead of
sustaining a powered cruise for hours.

78
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:02,030
None of those aircraft or
rockets was Project Pluto.

79
00:05:02,190 --> 00:05:05,705
Their historical footage belongs
here only as a comparison between

80
00:05:05,730 --> 00:05:09,710
technologies the United States was
developing during the same era.

81
00:05:10,070 --> 00:05:14,465
That comparison is crucial. In nineteen
fifty-seven, the future of nuclear

82
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:18,445
delivery was not self-evident. By
nineteen sixty-four, the comparison

83
00:05:18,470 --> 00:05:23,165
would look different. Every advance in
alternative systems changed the value

84
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:25,730
of making Pluto into
a deployable missile.

85
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:30,170
At the same time, Pluto's own
engineering demands remained enormous.

86
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:34,165
The scientists would spend the following
years answering the reactor part of

87
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:38,445
that question. And to get an answer,
they needed to build something in the

88
00:05:38,470 --> 00:05:42,585
Nevada desert that looked less
like an aircraft test stand than an

89
00:05:42,610 --> 00:05:43,770
industrial complex.

90
00:05:44,510 --> 00:05:49,105
At the Nevada Test Site, Project Pluto
occupied an industrial world of its

91
00:05:49,130 --> 00:05:53,750
own. The engine could not simply
be bolted to a stand, switched on,

92
00:05:54,050 --> 00:05:58,645
and treated like an ordinary motor. A
ramjet normally receives high-pressure

93
00:05:58,670 --> 00:06:02,330
airflow because it is moving at
enormous speed through the atmosphere.

94
00:06:02,890 --> 00:06:07,045
A reactor sitting on the ground has no
such airflow unless the test facility

95
00:06:07,070 --> 00:06:11,585
supplies it. That meant engineers had
to create, on the ground, part of the

96
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:14,830
environment the engine was
supposed to experience in flight.

97
00:06:15,330 --> 00:06:18,825
The solution involved massive
air-handling equipment, stored

98
00:06:18,850 --> 00:06:23,665
compressed air, temperature control,
instrumentation, remote operations,

99
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:27,230
and facilities for handling
equipment after a nuclear run.

100
00:06:27,670 --> 00:06:30,210
The test site did not
merely hold the engine.

101
00:06:30,310 --> 00:06:33,390
The test site temporarily stood
in for the missing aircraft.

102
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:38,225
The original engineering records explain
one basic limitation particularly

103
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:40,930
well. The air supply was finite.

104
00:06:41,390 --> 00:06:45,485
Contemporary Livermore engineers
explained that ground tests using stored

105
00:06:45,510 --> 00:06:49,665
air could last only a few minutes.
That limit was imposed by the test

106
00:06:49,690 --> 00:06:53,505
facility. It was not a measurement
of how long a nuclear-powered missile

107
00:06:53,530 --> 00:06:57,750
could theoretically fly. And
because these were nuclear reactors,

108
00:06:58,190 --> 00:07:01,625
handling the machinery after operation
raised questions that ordinary

109
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:06,465
aircraft testing rarely faced. The site
incorporated arrangements for remote

110
00:07:06,490 --> 00:07:08,890
work and post-test examination.

111
00:07:11,870 --> 00:07:13,865
Contemporary engineers even used a
remote-controlled railroad flatcar to

112
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:17,510
move the reactor between the test
position and the disassembly building.

113
00:07:17,570 --> 00:07:21,670
This was a real industrial railroad
built around a radioactive machine,

114
00:07:22,170 --> 00:07:26,725
not a missile launch system. The
Historic American Engineering Record

115
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:29,530
documents the dedicated
disassembly building.

116
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:34,005
Its purpose was to take apart tested
reactors so their components could be

117
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:38,745
studied and improved. Photographs made
for the federal historic survey show

118
00:07:38,770 --> 00:07:42,110
the real buildings and spaces
associated with the site.

119
00:07:42,230 --> 00:07:45,965
But they were recorded later. They
cannot honestly be presented as motion

120
00:07:45,990 --> 00:07:48,965
pictures of the tests occurring in
nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty

121
00:07:48,990 --> 00:07:53,450
four. The first major
experimental goal was Tory Two-A.

122
00:07:53,870 --> 00:07:57,705
It was a feasibility reactor, designed
to answer whether a nuclear ramjet

123
00:07:57,730 --> 00:08:01,865
core could withstand the relevant
heating and airflow while behaving in a

124
00:08:01,890 --> 00:08:06,585
controllable way. On May fourteenth,
nineteen sixty-one, Livermore recorded

125
00:08:06,610 --> 00:08:10,110
a forty-five-second test
of Tory Two-A at Nevada.

126
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:14,850
That forty-five-second result was not
a nuclear-powered missile taking off.

127
00:08:15,290 --> 00:08:18,945
It was a short, demanding demonstration
that a new type of reactor could

128
00:08:18,970 --> 00:08:22,305
operate under conditions chosen
to resemble part of its intended

129
00:08:22,330 --> 00:08:25,750
environment. It also exposed
what had to come next.

130
00:08:26,190 --> 00:08:29,725
A laboratory reactor arranged to
prove a principle is not automatically

131
00:08:29,750 --> 00:08:34,090
compact, durable, or controllable
enough to serve as a flight engine.

132
00:08:34,630 --> 00:08:37,610
The engineers had shown that
their basic idea was possible.

133
00:08:37,990 --> 00:08:42,225
Now they had to make the difficult part
smaller, tougher, and closer to the

134
00:08:42,250 --> 00:08:43,490
shape of an actual machine.

135
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:47,940
What made Project Pluto so
difficult was not the word nuclear.

136
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:52,075
It was the combination of nuclear
power with the physical demands of a

137
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:56,220
supersonic engine. Think about the
journey the air was supposed to take.

138
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:59,000
It would enter the proposed
vehicle at high speed.

139
00:08:59,460 --> 00:09:02,480
It would be directed through the
reactor's hot internal passages.

140
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:05,620
And it would leave with additional
energy, producing thrust.

141
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:09,695
For that process to matter, the reactor
had to transfer a tremendous amount

142
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,940
of heat to a moving stream of air.

143
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:16,135
But the hot surfaces could not be
allowed to simply collapse, crumble, or

144
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:18,340
deform when that air rushed past them.

145
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:21,900
Inside Tory's core,
ceramics played a central role.

146
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:24,980
This was not an abstract
material-science demonstration.

147
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:28,615
Engineers needed fuel-bearing
components with suitable nuclear

148
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:32,595
characteristics that could retain
their structure in a harsh thermal

149
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:36,980
environment. Livermore's own history
recalls the difficult combination:

150
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:41,515
structural strength, resistance to
damaging conditions, and the ability to

151
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:44,600
manufacture very large quantities
of consistent elements.

152
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,275
And a material that survives high
temperature in one setting may fail when

153
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:53,355
combined with intense airflow,
vibration, and the surrounding reactor

154
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:57,655
environment. That is why surviving
Project Pluto reports are filled with

155
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:02,595
drawings, test methods, and material
questions rather than dramatic pictures

156
00:10:02,620 --> 00:10:06,820
of missiles. Tory Two-A was built
to demonstrate the fundamentals.

157
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:11,180
Tory Two-C carried more of the burdens
that a missile engine would face.

158
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:15,235
Contemporary studies describe it as
intended to be adequate as an engine for

159
00:10:15,260 --> 00:10:19,315
a ramjet vehicle, and identify
control components within the reactor

160
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:23,480
environment as an especially difficult
step beyond the earlier test.

161
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:27,420
The control equipment has to function
when the environment around it is hot,

162
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:30,880
mechanically demanding, and
affected by the reactor itself.

163
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:35,495
Electronics, actuators, measurement
devices, and the systems that interpret

164
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:39,495
those readings therefore mattered
as much to the test campaign as the

165
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:44,155
reactor core. The nineteen-sixty-three
LASV-N1 film preserves

166
00:10:44,180 --> 00:10:47,780
some of that supporting development
world: technicians at apparatus,

167
00:10:48,220 --> 00:10:51,640
instrument racks, model tests,
and electronic research.

168
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:54,780
It is valuable evidence of
the engineering program.

169
00:10:54,940 --> 00:10:57,955
But a shot of a technician working
with control equipment is not

170
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:00,900
automatically a photograph
of Tory Two-C's core.

171
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:04,695
The reactor itself had moved
onto an entirely different scale.

172
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:08,955
A later Livermore history describes
Tory Two-C on the scale of five

173
00:11:08,980 --> 00:11:11,160
hundred megawatts of reactor heat.

174
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:14,420
It is a rounded figure, not
a precise test measurement.

175
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:17,640
And it measures thermal
power, not engine thrust.

176
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,540
The team was not merely building
a reactor that could turn on.

177
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:25,315
It was building a reactor intended
to turn a moving air stream into

178
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:29,295
propulsion, within the size and
reliability limits of a proposed high

179
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:33,675
speed missile. Before any such
vehicle could fly, the team had to

180
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:36,380
demonstrate that the engine
itself would survive.

181
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:40,620
In nineteen sixty-four, Tory
Two-C was ready for that test.

182
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:45,015
And the official record of what happened
next is much less ambiguous than

183
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:47,540
the explanations later
offered for why it ended.

184
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:52,775
By January of nineteen sixty-four,
workers had repaired the Nevada test

185
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:56,640
facility and resumed pre-nuclear
testing of its air systems.

186
00:11:56,900 --> 00:12:00,695
In mid-February, Tory Two-C was
shipped from Livermore to Nevada.

187
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:05,135
On March twenty-fifth, the reactor first
reached criticality while assembled

188
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:08,375
in its test vehicle. It was a
milestone, but not yet the complete

189
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,175
performance test. The next decisive
dates were May twelfth and May

190
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:16,075
twentieth. On May twelfth, the reactor
operated at an intermediate power

191
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:19,495
level. The conditions were intended
to simulate flight at Mach two point

192
00:12:19,520 --> 00:12:22,340
eight and an altitude
of ten thousand feet.

193
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,780
Eight days later came the
initial full-power ground test.

194
00:12:25,820 --> 00:12:29,275
The official account says that the
test conditions represented nuclear

195
00:12:29,300 --> 00:12:34,035
ramjet flight at the design speed
of Mach two point eight and at sea

196
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:38,795
level conditions. The Atomic Energy
Commission's report describes both May

197
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:43,475
tests as successful. Think about what
that means, and also what it does

198
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:48,555
not mean. The engineers had operated
a nuclear ramjet test reactor under

199
00:12:48,580 --> 00:12:52,955
deliberately chosen conditions intended
to reproduce significant demands of

200
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:56,340
flight. That was an extraordinary
experimental achievement.

201
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:59,360
But the reactor had remained
at the Nevada Test Site.

202
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:03,975
There was no flying SLAM vehicle. No
complete weapon had navigated a long

203
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:07,835
route. No aircraft structure had
demonstrated the full combination of

204
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:11,695
endurance, control, survivability,
and mission performance.

205
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:15,860
They also did not prove that the
proposed missile could now be deployed.

206
00:13:16,180 --> 00:13:19,820
The achievement belonged to a specific,
formidable engineering problem:

207
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:23,640
making a nuclear ramjet reactor
work at the test conditions.

208
00:13:23,900 --> 00:13:27,495
A nuclear rocket being tested for
Project Rover is not footage of Tory Two

209
00:13:27,520 --> 00:13:31,920
C. A missile launch from the
era is not a SLAM flight.

210
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:36,215
And a dramatic cloud above Nevada cannot
stand in for a reactor test merely

211
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:40,335
because it looks cinematic. The clearest
surviving evidence of the May tests

212
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:44,220
is found in the original reports and
photographs of the actual engine.

213
00:13:44,680 --> 00:13:46,880
Those documents have a
quieter kind of drama.

214
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:51,455
They record success. And that success
was followed, almost immediately,

215
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:55,560
by a decision that the program would
not reach the flight-test stage.

216
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,795
The engine had passed its examination.
The proposed weapon was about to lose

217
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,250
its purpose.

218
00:14:01,330 --> 00:14:05,945
On July first, nineteen sixty-four,
the Department of Defense informed the

219
00:14:05,970 --> 00:14:10,445
Atomic Energy Commission that it had
decided not to pursue Project Pluto's

220
00:14:10,470 --> 00:14:12,790
flight-test objective.
The language matters.

221
00:14:13,290 --> 00:14:17,065
The Atomic Energy Commission's account
does not describe a catastrophic

222
00:14:17,090 --> 00:14:21,485
reactor failure. Nor does it say that
the nuclear ramjet concept had been

223
00:14:21,510 --> 00:14:25,285
disproved. It says the Defense
Department would not continue toward

224
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:29,525
flight testing. The AEC responded
by canceling further planned reactor

225
00:14:29,550 --> 00:14:34,610
tests. The program was phased out,
and the Nevada facility was mothballed.

226
00:14:34,890 --> 00:14:38,425
That decision ended the path from
successful ground engine to an actual

227
00:14:38,450 --> 00:14:43,285
flying SLAM. What caused it? The funding
conflict was already visible before

228
00:14:43,310 --> 00:14:47,985
the July decision. In April, Congress's
Joint Committee on Atomic Energy cut

229
00:14:48,010 --> 00:14:51,750
proposed funding while pressing
for a definite flight-test plan.

230
00:14:52,210 --> 00:14:55,690
On June eleventh, the House
Appropriations Committee went further:

231
00:14:55,930 --> 00:15:00,005
it proposed sharply reducing further
Pluto spending, on the grounds that

232
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:03,245
there was still no approved military
system or requirement for the

233
00:15:03,270 --> 00:15:07,625
successful reactor. Those public
records show a concrete dispute over the

234
00:15:07,650 --> 00:15:09,910
next step, not a failed engine.

235
00:15:10,490 --> 00:15:14,245
They do not, by themselves, reveal
every private consideration behind the

236
00:15:14,270 --> 00:15:18,905
Pentagon's July decision. Later accounts
also discuss the rapid progress of

237
00:15:18,930 --> 00:15:22,885
ballistic missiles and the unresolved
operational, political, and

238
00:15:22,910 --> 00:15:26,145
environmental complications
of a flying nuclear ramjet.

239
00:15:26,170 --> 00:15:30,850
Those are historical explanations to
assess, not a proven single cause.

240
00:15:30,950 --> 00:15:35,245
Imagine the question asked in nineteen
fifty-seven: is it possible to create

241
00:15:35,270 --> 00:15:39,570
a nuclear ramjet capable of supporting
a long-range high-speed missile?

242
00:15:40,370 --> 00:15:44,485
Now imagine the question being asked
in nineteen sixty-four: do we still

243
00:15:44,510 --> 00:15:48,225
need this particular missile badly
enough to undertake everything required

244
00:15:48,250 --> 00:15:51,590
to make it operational? They
are not the same question.

245
00:15:52,010 --> 00:15:55,670
Once the test reactor works, the
remaining obligations become real.

246
00:15:56,070 --> 00:15:59,765
A flight program would have needed
a complete vehicle, its control and

247
00:15:59,790 --> 00:16:03,990
guidance systems, a way to launch
and recover useful test information,

248
00:16:04,190 --> 00:16:07,625
the operational infrastructure to
support it, and a military rationale

249
00:16:07,650 --> 00:16:10,665
strong enough to outweigh the
complications of a nuclear-powered

250
00:16:10,690 --> 00:16:14,625
cruise weapon. They made the next stage
a decision that would require a new

251
00:16:14,650 --> 00:16:18,265
military commitment. The Department
of Defense decided against the flight

252
00:16:18,290 --> 00:16:22,625
test objective. With further funding
already under pressure, that decision

253
00:16:22,650 --> 00:16:24,810
ended the route toward a flying SLAM.

254
00:16:25,690 --> 00:16:28,985
The comparison with the conventional
and ballistic missile systems of the

255
00:16:29,010 --> 00:16:32,150
period helps us understand
the broader strategic setting.

256
00:16:32,550 --> 00:16:36,485
But footage of Minuteman production
or an Atlas launch illustrates those

257
00:16:36,510 --> 00:16:40,765
alternatives, not the private reasoning
of the officials who decided Pluto's

258
00:16:40,790 --> 00:16:44,565
fate. The record leaves room for
discussion about how the different costs

259
00:16:44,590 --> 00:16:48,905
and risks were weighed. It leaves far
less room for doubt about the actual

260
00:16:48,930 --> 00:16:51,290
result. The reactor was tested.

261
00:16:51,730 --> 00:16:55,705
The missile never flew. And the
project that had spent years proving an

262
00:16:55,730 --> 00:16:59,405
engine could work was stopped before
it could prove the weapon was worth

263
00:16:59,430 --> 00:17:00,500
building.

264
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:04,615
Today, much of Project Pluto survives
in a form that is far less spectacular

265
00:17:04,640 --> 00:17:07,280
than the weapon its
designers once imagined.

266
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:11,020
There are engineering reports.
Historical photographs.

267
00:17:11,620 --> 00:17:14,995
Drawings that show how people approached
an unusual combination of reactor

268
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:19,375
physics and high-speed propulsion. And
there is the documented Nevada site

269
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:23,895
itself. The Historic American
Engineering Record identifies real Pluto

270
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:28,415
facilities in Area twenty-six and
records spaces used for test operations

271
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:32,515
and post-test disassembly. Those
photographs show the physical remains of

272
00:17:32,540 --> 00:17:36,175
a massive technical effort. They were
made after the program's main testing

273
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:39,935
period, and they belong to the story
as later evidence, not as invented

274
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:44,055
pictures of the moment of success.
Project Pluto never produced a flying

275
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:48,595
nuclear-powered SLAM missile. What it
produced was a demonstrated nuclear

276
00:17:48,620 --> 00:17:53,315
ramjet reactor, a body of engineering
experience, and a record of what an

277
00:17:53,340 --> 00:17:57,215
industrial project can accomplish
without ever becoming a useful weapons

278
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:01,700
system. One slogan says the
machine was too terrifying to use.

279
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:06,400
Another says the engineers built the
future and politicians threw it away.

280
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:09,440
Neither is an adequate
substitute for the evidence.

281
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:12,460
The people working on
Pluto solved real problems.

282
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:16,040
The Defense Department still
refused the flight-test objective.

283
00:18:16,420 --> 00:18:18,500
Both facts belong in the same story.

284
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:22,815
The most revealing image may be the
simplest: a real nuclear engine on the

285
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:25,735
ground, surrounded by the
equipment built to test it.

286
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:28,920
That was where America's
nuclear ramjet succeeded.

287
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:31,700
And it was also as far
as the missile ever got.

288
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:35,960
Project Pluto is not the story
of a weapon that failed its test.

289
00:18:36,340 --> 00:18:39,935
It is the story of an engine that
passed — and a weapon that never made it

290
00:18:39,960 --> 00:18:41,560
beyond the decision to build one.

